WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.359
Lesson four point five.

2
00:00:01.704 --> 00:00:04.457
S4D: how a state-space model steps through

3
00:00:04.457 --> 00:00:06.867
slots, and two ways to compute the same

4
00:00:06.867 --> 00:00:07.326
numbers.

5
00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:13.274
Lesson four point four ended on x prime

6
00:00:13.274 --> 00:00:15.358
equals A x plus B u, in continuous time.

7
00:00:15.656 --> 00:00:17.590
Inside S128, at the lit stage of the

8
00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:19.476
strip, each block has a hundred and

9
00:00:19.476 --> 00:00:21.708
twenty-eight channels, and each channel is

10
00:00:21.708 --> 00:00:23.792
its own small state-space model: one

11
00:00:23.792 --> 00:00:25.875
number in, one number out, every slot.

12
00:00:26.173 --> 00:00:28.356
From here on the state is called s, as in

13
00:00:28.356 --> 00:00:28.802
the code.

14
00:00:29.221 --> 00:00:31.094
The music doesn't arrive as a smooth line.

15
00:00:31.362 --> 00:00:32.432
It arrives slot by slot.

16
00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.573
So we only need the state at the slots,

17
00:00:34.573 --> 00:00:36.268
and a rule that steps from one to the

18
00:00:36.268 --> 00:00:36.491
next.

19
00:00:36.759 --> 00:00:38.052
That's called discretisation.

20
00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:40.282
Take four point four's bucket, and a step

21
00:00:40.282 --> 00:00:41.040
of half a second.

22
00:00:41.308 --> 00:00:43.181
With the tap off, every step multiplies

23
00:00:43.181 --> 00:00:45.009
the level by the same number, e to the

24
00:00:45.009 --> 00:00:46.793
minus a half times a half, zero point

25
00:00:46.793 --> 00:00:47.864
seven seven eight eight.

26
00:00:48.325 --> 00:00:50.904
In general, that number is A bar, e to the

27
00:00:50.904 --> 00:00:51.892
step size times A.

28
00:00:52.222 --> 00:00:54.362
The step size is called dt in the code.

29
00:00:54.691 --> 00:00:57.216
And here is the twist: dt is not a fixed

30
00:00:57.216 --> 00:00:58.204
number of seconds.

31
00:00:58.533 --> 00:01:01.003
It is a trained number, one per channel,

32
00:01:01.003 --> 00:01:02.924
saying how much of the model's own

33
00:01:02.924 --> 00:01:04.735
continuous clock one slot covers.

34
00:01:06.198 --> 00:01:08.885
Now the real thing: S128, block one,

35
00:01:08.885 --> 00:01:09.762
channel thirty.

36
00:01:10.112 --> 00:01:12.683
Its trained step: zero point zero zero

37
00:01:12.683 --> 00:01:13.325
three five.

38
00:01:13.676 --> 00:01:16.304
Its first mode's rate: minus zero point

39
00:01:16.304 --> 00:01:18.466
two one minus zero point five four i.

40
00:01:18.817 --> 00:01:20.335
Times the step: tiny.

41
00:01:20.686 --> 00:01:23.490
The real part gives the shrink: zero point

42
00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:24.892
nine nine nine two five.

43
00:01:25.243 --> 00:01:27.930
The imaginary part gives the turn: under

44
00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:29.566
two thousandths of a radian.

45
00:01:29.916 --> 00:01:32.837
Together, A bar: each slot this mode keeps

46
00:01:32.837 --> 00:01:35.057
ninety-nine point nine percent of its

47
00:01:35.057 --> 00:01:36.517
size, and turns a hair.

48
00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:42.417
One more question: what does the input do

49
00:01:42.417 --> 00:01:43.369
between two slots?

50
00:01:43.686 --> 00:01:46.064
S4D holds it flat for the whole step, like

51
00:01:46.064 --> 00:01:46.698
a staircase.

52
00:01:47.015 --> 00:01:48.706
That's called a zero-order hold.

53
00:01:49.191 --> 00:01:51.466
Hold the input flat, solve the bucket's

54
00:01:51.466 --> 00:01:53.850
equation across one step, and you get the

55
00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.505
textbook step: the old state times A bar,

56
00:01:56.505 --> 00:01:58.943
plus the input times B bar, where B bar is

57
00:01:58.943 --> 00:02:00.948
A bar minus one, over A, times B.

58
00:02:01.273 --> 00:02:03.549
For the bucket with a step of a half, B

59
00:02:03.549 --> 00:02:05.066
bar is zero point four four.

60
00:02:05.523 --> 00:02:07.334
Our code does something slightly

61
00:02:07.334 --> 00:02:09.638
different, and it matters when you read

62
00:02:09.638 --> 00:02:09.802
it.

63
00:02:10.132 --> 00:02:12.161
Its step is just A bar times the old

64
00:02:12.161 --> 00:02:13.478
state, plus the input.

65
00:02:13.807 --> 00:02:14.301
No B bar.

66
00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:16.770
The B bar factor moves to the read-out

67
00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:19.129
instead: C prime equals C times A bar

68
00:02:19.129 --> 00:02:20.226
minus one, over A.

69
00:02:20.555 --> 00:02:22.750
That's allowed because each part of the

70
00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:25.273
state is on its own: scale a state up by

71
00:02:25.273 --> 00:02:27.468
any number and its read-out down by the

72
00:02:27.468 --> 00:02:29.552
same, and the output doesn't change.

73
00:02:29.881 --> 00:02:32.240
So our state is the textbook state divided

74
00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:32.734
by B bar.

75
00:02:34.008 --> 00:02:35.503
Channel thirty's first mode.

76
00:02:35.823 --> 00:02:38.118
Its factor, A bar minus one over A, is

77
00:02:38.118 --> 00:02:40.360
zero point zero zero three five, almost

78
00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.655
exactly the step dt, because the step is

79
00:02:42.655 --> 00:02:43.136
so small.

80
00:02:43.456 --> 00:02:45.484
Its trained C is minus one point four

81
00:02:45.484 --> 00:02:47.246
seven plus zero point five two i.

82
00:02:47.566 --> 00:02:49.915
So C prime is C times that: minus zero

83
00:02:49.915 --> 00:02:52.050
point zero zero five one four plus zero

84
00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:53.758
point zero zero one eight one i.

85
00:02:54.232 --> 00:02:56.401
In the code: four lines, then two.

86
00:02:56.718 --> 00:02:58.993
The step dt, the rate A, their product.

87
00:02:59.311 --> 00:03:01.956
C prime, with the hold's factor folded in;

88
00:03:01.956 --> 00:03:03.172
the comment says Z O H.

89
00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:06.029
And the step: A bar times s plus u, then

90
00:03:06.029 --> 00:03:08.198
two times the real part of C prime times

91
00:03:08.198 --> 00:03:09.468
s, summed, plus D u.

92
00:03:09.785 --> 00:03:11.055
The doubling comes next.

93
00:03:14.407 --> 00:03:15.866
Why can S4D afford so many states?

94
00:03:16.123 --> 00:03:17.711
A full A lets every state feed every

95
00:03:17.711 --> 00:03:19.514
other: for sixty-four states, four

96
00:03:19.514 --> 00:03:21.402
thousand and ninety-six multiplies, every

97
00:03:21.402 --> 00:03:22.346
channel, every slot.

98
00:03:22.604 --> 00:03:24.578
S4D's A is diagonal: numbers only on the

99
00:03:24.578 --> 00:03:24.964
diagonal.

100
00:03:25.222 --> 00:03:27.067
Each part of the state, called a mode,

101
00:03:27.067 --> 00:03:28.612
then changes on its own, with one

102
00:03:28.612 --> 00:03:28.999
multiply.

103
00:03:29.256 --> 00:03:31.102
Most systems can be written this way, as

104
00:03:31.102 --> 00:03:33.033
four point four did for the spring; the

105
00:03:33.033 --> 00:03:34.449
code simply chooses a diagonal A.

106
00:03:36.134 --> 00:03:38.103
So one channel's layer is two lines.

107
00:03:38.431 --> 00:03:40.784
The state, thirty-two complex numbers,

108
00:03:40.784 --> 00:03:43.136
each times its own A bar, plus the input.

109
00:03:43.464 --> 00:03:45.871
The output: C prime times the state,

110
00:03:45.871 --> 00:03:48.059
summed over the modes, twice the real

111
00:03:48.059 --> 00:03:48.989
part, plus D u.

112
00:03:49.317 --> 00:03:51.559
Why thirty-two, and why twice the real

113
00:03:51.559 --> 00:03:51.833
part?

114
00:03:52.161 --> 00:03:54.459
Four point four's spring had its rates in

115
00:03:54.459 --> 00:03:55.170
mirror pairs.

116
00:03:55.498 --> 00:03:57.850
Any system whose A holds only real numbers

117
00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:00.202
has its complex rates in mirror pairs like

118
00:04:00.202 --> 00:04:02.609
that, and the two halves of a pair add to

119
00:04:02.609 --> 00:04:04.086
twice the real part of one.

120
00:04:04.414 --> 00:04:06.985
So S4D keeps one of each pair: thirty-two

121
00:04:06.985 --> 00:04:08.626
modes standing for sixty-four.

122
00:04:09.067 --> 00:04:11.023
Each block: a hundred and twenty-eight

123
00:04:11.023 --> 00:04:12.668
channels of thirty-two modes, four

124
00:04:12.668 --> 00:04:14.447
thousand and ninety-six complex numbers.

125
00:04:14.714 --> 00:04:16.670
Four blocks: thirty-two thousand seven

126
00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:18.671
hundred and sixty-eight numbers of memory,

127
00:04:18.671 --> 00:04:20.361
the size lesson three point four gave.

128
00:04:20.628 --> 00:04:22.273
Inside this layer the channels never

129
00:04:22.273 --> 00:04:24.319
touch; they meet in the LayerNorm before

130
00:04:24.319 --> 00:04:26.186
it and in the linear layer and gate after

131
00:04:26.186 --> 00:04:27.120
it, four point six.

132
00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:32.068
Training needs a starting point.

133
00:04:32.384 --> 00:04:34.917
S4D's is called S4D-Lin: every mode starts

134
00:04:34.917 --> 00:04:37.132
with the rate minus a half plus i pi n,

135
00:04:37.132 --> 00:04:39.084
four point four's spiral, and each

136
00:04:39.084 --> 00:04:41.352
channel's step dt starts somewhere between

137
00:04:41.352 --> 00:04:43.462
zero point zero zero one and zero point

138
00:04:43.462 --> 00:04:43.673
one.

139
00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:46.205
Together that means every mode starts out

140
00:04:46.205 --> 00:04:48.262
remembering for between twenty and two

141
00:04:48.262 --> 00:04:49.054
thousand slots.

142
00:04:49.483 --> 00:04:51.735
The rates and the step are trained, like C

143
00:04:51.735 --> 00:04:52.035
and D.

144
00:04:52.335 --> 00:04:54.186
Here are channel thirty's thirty-two

145
00:04:54.186 --> 00:04:54.486
rates.

146
00:04:54.786 --> 00:04:56.838
At the start they sat in a straight line

147
00:04:56.838 --> 00:04:58.589
at minus a half, spaced pi apart.

148
00:04:58.889 --> 00:05:01.390
After training: the up-and-down positions,

149
00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:03.442
the frequencies, stayed close to where

150
00:05:03.442 --> 00:05:05.693
they began, and the decay rates spread out

151
00:05:05.693 --> 00:05:06.143
sideways.

152
00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:08.541
Across all sixteen thousand modes, the

153
00:05:08.541 --> 00:05:08.785
same.

154
00:05:09.078 --> 00:05:11.176
The imaginary parts moved a median of zero

155
00:05:11.176 --> 00:05:11.957
point two three.

156
00:05:12.249 --> 00:05:14.348
The decay rates spread to a median of zero

157
00:05:14.348 --> 00:05:16.446
point four four, most between zero point

158
00:05:16.446 --> 00:05:17.959
two two and zero point six six.

159
00:05:18.252 --> 00:05:20.155
Ninety-four percent still remember for

160
00:05:20.155 --> 00:05:22.302
twenty to two thousand slots; training

161
00:05:22.302 --> 00:05:24.205
pushed a few hundred shorter and a few

162
00:05:24.205 --> 00:05:24.937
hundred longer.

163
00:05:28.273 --> 00:05:29.508
How long does a mode remember?

164
00:05:29.755 --> 00:05:31.361
Its size shrinks by A bar's size every

165
00:05:31.361 --> 00:05:33.172
slot, so it falls to one over e after tau

166
00:05:33.172 --> 00:05:35.024
slots, where tau is minus one over the log

167
00:05:35.024 --> 00:05:35.683
of A bar's size.

168
00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:37.906
For channel thirty's first mode: about one

169
00:05:37.906 --> 00:05:39.470
thousand three hundred and thirty-two

170
00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:39.717
slots.

171
00:05:39.964 --> 00:05:41.652
At a hundred and twenty beats a minute a

172
00:05:41.652 --> 00:05:43.052
slot is a hundred and twenty-five

173
00:05:43.052 --> 00:05:44.986
milliseconds, so that's eighty-three bars,

174
00:05:44.986 --> 00:05:46.427
or about two minutes and forty-six

175
00:05:46.427 --> 00:05:46.756
seconds.

176
00:05:47.101 --> 00:05:50.011
Now all sixteen thousand three hundred and

177
00:05:50.011 --> 00:05:52.989
eighty-four modes of S128, counted by how

178
00:05:52.989 --> 00:05:54.275
long they remember.

179
00:05:54.845 --> 00:05:56.891
The middle mode remembers for two hundred

180
00:05:56.891 --> 00:05:58.986
and thirty-three slots: about fifteen

181
00:05:58.986 --> 00:06:00.350
bars, twenty-nine seconds.

182
00:06:00.643 --> 00:06:02.543
One in ten remembers for more than one

183
00:06:02.543 --> 00:06:04.541
thousand three hundred and seventy-eight

184
00:06:04.541 --> 00:06:05.759
slots, eighty-six bars.

185
00:06:06.051 --> 00:06:07.756
Five hundred and seventy-six modes

186
00:06:07.756 --> 00:06:09.803
remember for longer than two thousand and

187
00:06:09.803 --> 00:06:11.898
forty-eight slots, longer than the whole

188
00:06:11.898 --> 00:06:12.482
worked song.

189
00:06:12.883 --> 00:06:13.840
Two honest limits.

190
00:06:14.159 --> 00:06:16.234
These times are exact for the layer,

191
00:06:16.234 --> 00:06:18.468
because A bar never depends on what comes

192
00:06:18.468 --> 00:06:18.627
in.

193
00:06:18.946 --> 00:06:20.968
Whether the slow modes carry anything

194
00:06:20.968 --> 00:06:22.564
musical has not been measured.

195
00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:28.760
Back to the worked slot: validation song

196
00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:31.405
zero zero one one b three c f, slot five

197
00:06:31.405 --> 00:06:33.988
hundred and seventy-six, where the real

198
00:06:33.988 --> 00:06:36.511
drummer plays kick and closed hat, and

199
00:06:36.511 --> 00:06:37.372
S128 does too.

200
00:06:37.741 --> 00:06:40.140
Channel thirty's input at this slot is

201
00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:41.801
five point seven five nine.

202
00:06:42.324 --> 00:06:42.788
Mode zero.

203
00:06:43.066 --> 00:06:45.013
Its state after slot five seventy-five is

204
00:06:45.013 --> 00:06:46.867
two thousand two hundred and fifty-four

205
00:06:46.867 --> 00:06:48.860
minus one thousand two hundred and fifteen

206
00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:48.953
i.

207
00:06:49.231 --> 00:06:51.410
Times A bar: shrunk a little and turned a

208
00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:51.734
little.

209
00:06:52.012 --> 00:06:52.708
Plus the input.

210
00:06:52.986 --> 00:06:54.887
The input is a real number, so it only

211
00:06:54.887 --> 00:06:55.814
moves the real part.

212
00:06:56.092 --> 00:06:58.224
Read out with C prime and doubled: minus

213
00:06:58.224 --> 00:06:59.476
eighteen point seven eight.

214
00:06:59.871 --> 00:07:02.209
Mode four, the same four moves: two point

215
00:07:02.209 --> 00:07:02.925
zero eight six.

216
00:07:03.396 --> 00:07:05.932
Do that for all thirty-two modes and add:

217
00:07:05.932 --> 00:07:07.550
minus eight point eight three.

218
00:07:07.874 --> 00:07:10.301
Plus the skip, D times u: minus eight

219
00:07:10.301 --> 00:07:11.219
point five three.

220
00:07:11.542 --> 00:07:13.916
The layer's output for channel thirty:

221
00:07:13.916 --> 00:07:15.642
minus seventeen point three six.

222
00:07:16.073 --> 00:07:17.809
Mode zero's state reads two thousand five

223
00:07:17.809 --> 00:07:19.132
hundred and sixty-three in size.

224
00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:21.157
Part of that is the code's convention from

225
00:07:21.157 --> 00:07:23.265
chapter two: leaving out B bar, about dt,

226
00:07:23.265 --> 00:07:24.959
scales the state up by about one over dt.

227
00:07:25.207 --> 00:07:26.654
In textbook units it is about nine.

228
00:07:26.902 --> 00:07:28.679
Either way it holds every earlier input,

229
00:07:28.679 --> 00:07:30.332
each shrunk and turned since it arrived.

230
00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:32.482
One more fact, for four point six: the

231
00:07:32.482 --> 00:07:34.217
GELU right after this layer turns channel

232
00:07:34.217 --> 00:07:35.209
thirty off at this slot.

233
00:07:35.457 --> 00:07:36.325
Memory is not output.

234
00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:41.858
Now write the step out, starting from an

235
00:07:41.858 --> 00:07:42.477
empty state.

236
00:07:42.786 --> 00:07:45.056
After two slots: A bar times the first

237
00:07:45.056 --> 00:07:46.345
input, plus the second.

238
00:07:46.655 --> 00:07:48.821
After three: A bar squared times the

239
00:07:48.821 --> 00:07:51.090
first, A bar times the second, plus the

240
00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:51.400
third.

241
00:07:51.709 --> 00:07:54.133
In general, the state is every past input,

242
00:07:54.133 --> 00:07:56.248
times A bar to the power of how long ago

243
00:07:56.248 --> 00:07:56.661
it came.

244
00:07:57.142 --> 00:07:59.609
Put that into the read-out, and the output

245
00:07:59.609 --> 00:08:01.912
is a sum over past inputs, each times a

246
00:08:01.912 --> 00:08:04.270
weight K that depends only on how far back

247
00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:04.599
it is.

248
00:08:04.928 --> 00:08:07.176
K at lag l is two times the real part of

249
00:08:07.176 --> 00:08:09.369
the sum of C prime times A bar to the l.

250
00:08:09.698 --> 00:08:12.001
That list of weights is the kernel, and

251
00:08:12.001 --> 00:08:13.975
summing past inputs against it is a

252
00:08:13.975 --> 00:08:14.633
convolution.

253
00:08:15.096 --> 00:08:17.256
Channel thirty's real kernel, the first

254
00:08:17.256 --> 00:08:18.850
two hundred and fifty-six lags.

255
00:08:19.158 --> 00:08:21.472
The biggest weight is at lag eight, half a

256
00:08:21.472 --> 00:08:23.272
bar back: zero point one four.

257
00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:25.843
Then it swings, because the modes turn,

258
00:08:25.843 --> 00:08:28.054
and it is far from dead at lag two hundred

259
00:08:28.054 --> 00:08:28.774
and fifty-six.

260
00:08:29.082 --> 00:08:31.242
Once trained, it is the same kernel for

261
00:08:31.242 --> 00:08:31.807
every song.

262
00:08:35.197 --> 00:08:37.180
Both the trainer's validation loss and the

263
00:08:37.180 --> 00:08:38.887
scorer walk a song in chunks of five

264
00:08:38.887 --> 00:08:40.870
hundred and twelve slots, with the state

265
00:08:40.870 --> 00:08:41.562
carried across.

266
00:08:41.839 --> 00:08:43.545
Slot five hundred and seventy-six is

267
00:08:43.545 --> 00:08:45.252
sixty-five slots into the chunk that

268
00:08:45.252 --> 00:08:46.820
starts at five hundred and twelve.

269
00:08:47.097 --> 00:08:49.127
Inside the chunk: sixty-five inputs to

270
00:08:49.127 --> 00:08:50.280
convolve with the kernel.

271
00:08:50.556 --> 00:08:52.540
Everything before the chunk arrives as the

272
00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:54.477
state carried in at slot five hundred and

273
00:08:54.477 --> 00:08:54.800
eleven.

